{:edb/db-before-t 1421 :edb/db-after-t 1422 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534734 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:26:26.416Z" 13194139534734 true] [17592186171771 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/2d6f4a939a2c25d1706a7d54a323af2773d30ddb08bcfcada94466bb8d1b9ef9.png)\n\nSketch the slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y-1$ at the points indicated in the diagram above." 13194139534734 true] [17592186171771 1100 "Sketch the slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y-1$ at the points indicated in the diagram above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/fa81fd3beb39de6abd0fa2d7b68f9194.png)" 13194139534734 false] [17592186171771 1145 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,1), (-2,1)$, and $(-2,2)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(2,1)}&=2(1)-1=1,\\\\\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(-2,1)}&=2(1)-1=1,\\\\\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(-2,2)}&=2(2)-1=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/1f61f2a6d4602e7660245b212c064045.png)" 13194139534734 false] [17592186171771 1145 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,1), (-2,1),$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=2(1)-1=1 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=2(1)-1=1 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,2)}=2(2)-1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/be6bc036e194683af09a5936d433d7d45c7532e06054d9d473f3b433167ea102.png)" 13194139534734 true] [17592186171772 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3y-1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y = -1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary\n\n- At every point along the line $x = 0,$ the slope of the slope field varies\n\n- At every point along the line $y = 0,$ the slope field is horizontal" 13194139534734 true] [17592186171772 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3y-1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point along the line $y = -1$, the slope of the slope field does not vary\n2. At every point along the line $x = 0$, the slope of the slope field varies\n3. At every point along the line $y = 0$, the slope field is horizontal" 13194139534734 false] [17592186171772 1145 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y$, the slope does not vary with $x$.\n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,-1)} = 3(-1)-1 = -4$,\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-4$ for any $x$.\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(0,y)} = 3y-1$,\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ depends on $y$.\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,0)} = 3(0)-1=-1 \\neq 0$,\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is $-1$ for any $x$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/23f047df3d0fa3e0d11f9be81ec6d279.png)" 13194139534734 false] [17592186171772 1145 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,-1)} = 3(-1)-1 = -4,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-4$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(0,y)} = 3y-1,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ depends on $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,0)} = 3(0)-1=-1 \\neq 0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is $-1$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/f3ca77530d9126d733d33dc01ccee82d2740f789dcf712c67f03139fb09b753b.png)" 13194139534734 true] [17592186171773 1100 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/ce8ff84e785ad9edbbd56ef02d4e0ed8.png)" 13194139534734 false] [17592186171773 1100 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/3990c49349d446512d59f1f722506ea7337143eba6ec49f29dc87af060fb553b.png)" 13194139534734 true] [17592186171773 1145 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y$, the slope does not vary with $x$.\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/fe3aa4398d5f839f6c68b1b1a7fa3b5d.png)\n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we move left and right along the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/3ac23747a88c5d438abce91c471b6228.png)" 13194139534734 false] [17592186171773 1145 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/df3dd6c7db3e88ac2ac2a0ad82b19e5cac427f69ddf0eeba841445612e62ec4e.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes 