bitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=-2+1=-1<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=-1+1=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=1+1=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/caa69f6116eba6462c4fe18f2e535d83db350f0797cf1fcd895ac6df9a4d6fd7.png)" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306467 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306468 13194139534734 true] [17592186306467 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306469 13194139534734 true] [17592186306467 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306470 13194139534734 true] [17592186306467 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306471 13194139534734 true] [17592186306467 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306472 13194139534734 true] [17592186306467 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534734 true] [17592186306467 1041 "selection" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306467 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306472 13194139534734 true] [17592186306467 1110 #uuid "401e099b-6c93-5cc5-b46b-ae89e91f9358" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306468 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306468 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534734 true] [17592186306468 1111 #uuid "510ac373-dd16-5f71-bec0-eb88c7be68f2" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306469 1034 "III only" 13194139534734 true