(x,-1)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=2(0)=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=2(1)=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/69065d8e064b0fd679f059925f5445dcb2c7d037f180d3a144ee527ea408e8db.png)" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306475 13194139534734 true] [17592186306474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306476 13194139534734 true] [17592186306474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306477 13194139534734 true] [17592186306474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306478 13194139534734 true] [17592186306474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306479 13194139534734 true] [17592186306474 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534734 true] [17592186306474 1041 "selection" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306474 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306475 13194139534734 true] [17592186306474 1110 #uuid "e40053a9-3249-5479-b63b-cbf93da09373" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306475 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306475 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534734 true] [17592186306475 1111 #uuid "9d85016a-c3df-5e1b-ba4e-a3c946cb9687" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306476 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534734 true] [17592186306476 1111 #uuid "714c6d42-b007-5534-ba37-1