rue. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=(0)^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=(1)^{2}=1>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $1$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/0c6e2e486ae770bc99ef9fe41f02d224de65119ade910d303ad3f57f12e8d84b.png)" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306482 13194139534734 true] [17592186306481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306483 13194139534734 true] [17592186306481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306484 13194139534734 true] [17592186306481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306485 13194139534734 true] [17592186306481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306486 13194139534734 true] [17592186306481 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534734 true] [17592186306481 1041 "selection" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306481 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306482 13194139534734 true] [17592186306481 1110 #uuid "1367ef06-b74c-5d12-b678-36ebf0cba3cf" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306482 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306482 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534734 true] [17592186306482 1111 #uuid "439722bb-d102-546e-862c-957d0394576a" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306483 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306483 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534734 true] [17592186306483 1111 #uuid "f5ac134f-8b7c-5847-afc2-9c3ec5012a27" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306484 1034 "III only" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306484 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534734 true] [17592186306484 1111 #uuid "afb4f72b-27e4-5e75-8e43-f8971796cf5f" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306485 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306485 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534734 true] [17592186306485 1111 #uuid "e6f4b3a9-7ad1-52a6-8141-558c25bb5c1a" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306486 1034 "II only" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306486 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534734 true] [17592186306486 1111 #uuid "91b37e25-54fc-50da-a8aa-0a733deca278" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306487 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306488 13194139534734 true] [17592186306487 1089 "q-97292" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306487 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534734 true] [17592186306487 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary\n- At every point along the line $x=1,$ the slope of the slope field varies\n- At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306487 1113 #uuid "7a0a3c6e-b7b2-5bdb-b099-81675d5f04cb" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306487 1145 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=-(-1)^{2}=-1,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,y)}=-{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(1,y)$ depends on $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=-(0)^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/272427db87d74e3d4ae09dc69b7add53e8def9ead184a1ab90afc99fc0a7628e.png)" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306488 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306489 13194139534734 true] [17592186306488 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306490 13194139534734 true] [17592186306488 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306491 13194139534734 true] [17592186306488 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306492 13194139534734 true] [17592186306488 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306493 13194139534734 true] [17592186306488 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534734 true] [17592186306488 1041 "selection" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306488 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306489 13194139534734 true] [17592186306488 1110 #uuid "733b35b8-eaab-5ef6-85d9-6635605f3f8c" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306489 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306489 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534734 true] [17592186306489 1111 #uuid "b5f99fb6-df4e-5ea2-bd6e-4bc40164a6b7" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306490 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306490 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534734 true] [17592186306490 1111 #uuid "e3d7969f-317d-567f-8fbb-37baf68aaca2" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306491 1034 "III only" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306491 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534734 true] [17592186306491 1111 #uuid "bb774822-2229-5471-b85e-ed711cecd7cd" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306492 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534734 true] [17592186306492 1103 #edb/ref 17592186